00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
It's time to dive into NAT evaluation's framework

00:00:02.508 --> 00:00:04.799
to measure agent and workflow performance.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.900
You'll create datasets with ground truth,

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.800
uncover bugs, and use insights

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.698
to make data-driven improvements with confidence.

00:00:11.702 --> 00:00:14.202
Let's get to it. In this lesson,

00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:16.503
we're going to learn about agentic evaluation.

00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:19.503
Evaluation is super important for agents.

00:00:19.503 --> 00:00:23.402
Evaluation can often be overlooked in a complex agentic environment.

00:00:23.419 --> 00:00:27.228
NAT makes it straightforward to run evaluations against your workflow.

00:00:27.669 --> 00:00:30.002
So in this lesson, we will create

00:00:30.002 --> 00:00:32.668
evaluation data sets of question and answer pairs,

00:00:32.868 --> 00:00:34.739
and we'll use that data set to test if

00:00:34.739 --> 00:00:36.609
our agent is actually giving us a correct answer.

00:00:37.319 --> 00:00:39.013
And along the way, we will discover a

00:00:39.013 --> 00:00:40.918
bug in a production workflow and we'll fix it.

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:43.334
Why would you evaluate your agent?

00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:47.234
Often times, we'll ship something into production and it'll work.

00:00:47.634 --> 00:00:50.033
But something can drift and we might not catch it.

00:00:50.088 --> 00:00:52.887
Maybe we update a model or maybe we change a prompt.

00:00:53.088 --> 00:00:55.588
Evaluations help keep our agents honest.

00:00:56.287 --> 00:00:58.887
NAT has several evaluation frameworks built in.

00:00:59.088 --> 00:01:01.987
Of course, it's pluggable, so you can add your own evaluation framework

00:01:01.987 --> 00:01:03.887
if you have one that you like.

00:01:04.078 --> 00:01:06.879
But it's just a simple matter of adding evaluation properties

00:01:06.879 --> 00:01:10.578
to your configuration YAML and running NAT Eval.

00:01:11.078 --> 00:01:13.578
So what is NAT's evaluation flow?

00:01:13.879 --> 00:01:15.478
You give it a dataset with

00:01:15.478 --> 00:01:17.078
a list of inputs and expected outputs.

00:01:17.478 --> 00:01:20.078
and NAT will run those evals against your agentic system

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:21.532
and it will score how many

00:01:21.532 --> 00:01:22.879
were correct and how many were wrong.

00:01:23.278 --> 00:01:25.578
Of course, you might have your own evaluation framework

00:01:25.578 --> 00:01:28.078
that can't be expressed using input and output pairs.

00:01:28.478 --> 00:01:30.578
NAT's eval system is pluggable,

00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:33.879
so you can just create a wrapper around your own evaluation framework.

00:01:34.403 --> 00:01:36.004
What does this look like in config?

00:01:36.403 --> 00:01:38.065
So we have a new top-level

00:01:38.065 --> 00:01:40.004
property for our configuration file called eval.

00:01:40.703 --> 00:01:43.703
And this eval will take a few other properties.

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:45.903
The general property is where we

00:01:45.903 --> 00:01:48.004
tell it where to dump its output

00:01:48.168 --> 00:01:50.269
and where to load the datasets from.

00:01:50.769 --> 00:01:52.968
And you can configure one or more evaluators.

00:01:53.468 --> 00:01:56.968
In our case, we have one evaluator and we've named it answer_accuracy.

00:01:57.168 --> 00:01:59.418
And it is a ragas evaluator, which is

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:01.668
an evaluation framework you might be familiar with.

00:02:01.968 --> 00:02:03.835
And it's built into NAT. Ragas has

00:02:03.835 --> 00:02:05.968
many different metrics that you can pick from.

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:07.868
In this case, we've picked AnswerAccuracy.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:10.269
You can see the other metrics in our documentation.

00:02:10.508 --> 00:02:12.609
and we've handed it an LLM.

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:14.509
You can see our question and answer

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:16.308
pair here, the average temperature in Austria.

00:02:16.808 --> 00:02:20.508
And we pass that into the simple eval JSON dataset.

00:02:20.717 --> 00:02:22.516
and then we call nat eval.

00:02:22.907 --> 00:02:25.507
And this is the power of Config-Driven Development.

00:02:25.817 --> 00:02:27.667
Instead of changing code, rewriting a lot

00:02:27.667 --> 00:02:29.516
of Python, hoping we don't break it.

00:02:29.817 --> 00:02:31.717
We can do experiments in our config.

00:02:31.926 --> 00:02:34.526
We can change functions. We can swap out LLMs.

00:02:35.127 --> 00:02:36.826
If we have our eval set up correctly,

00:02:36.926 --> 00:02:38.926
we can make these changes and run our evals

00:02:39.026 --> 00:02:41.326
and be confident that our changes are correct.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:43.609
Of course, you can add more than one

00:02:43.609 --> 00:02:45.727
evaluation to your config, as many as you like.

00:02:46.127 --> 00:02:47.340
And you can use them in tandem

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:48.727
with other features that we've already looked at,

00:02:49.127 --> 00:02:50.727
such as observability.

00:02:51.526 --> 00:02:53.050
You could run your evaluations in

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.826
parallel while they're hooked up to Phoenix,

00:02:54.926 --> 00:02:56.080
and you can watch the trace

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.426
data come in as the evals run.

00:02:57.979 --> 00:03:00.241
Without observability, we don't know how

00:03:00.241 --> 00:03:02.879
our agent arrived at a correct answer.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.118
Without evaluations, we don't know if

00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:07.379
our agent arrived at a correct answer.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.879
With NAT's evaluation framework,

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:12.780
we can score multiple evaluation question and answer pairs

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.580
and be confident that our agent is ready to go.

00:03:14.817 --> 00:03:17.546
In this notebook, we are going to look at evaluation.

00:03:17.847 --> 00:03:21.187
How do we find and fix bugs with NeMo Agent Toolkit?

00:03:21.486 --> 00:03:24.497
Let's go ahead and install the climate_analyzer package

00:03:24.497 --> 00:03:26.616
that we've been working with and run some evals against it.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:30.120
Now that that's installed, let's look at our evaluation dataset.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.819
So we have a very small evaluation dataset here.

00:03:33.219 --> 00:03:35.120
It contains one question and one answer.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.919
What was the average temperature in Austria in 1980?

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:43.020
and the average temperature in Austria in 1980 was 6.8

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:45.782
degrees Celsius. Let's find what our

00:03:45.782 --> 00:03:48.538
climate data says about Austria in 1980.

00:03:49.538 --> 00:03:51.638
We get some values back here. Let's

00:03:51.638 --> 00:03:54.038
calculate the average to confirm our ground truth.

00:03:55.038 --> 00:03:56.423
And we see from our climate

00:03:56.423 --> 00:03:58.038
data that the average temperature for Austria

00:03:58.038 --> 00:04:01.038
in 1980 was 6.8 degrees Celsius.

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:03.739
How do we run evaluations in NAT?

00:04:04.128 --> 00:04:07.329
Let's look at a pre-written evaluation config file to see.

00:04:07.729 --> 00:04:09.329
This file should be familiar to you.

00:04:09.729 --> 00:04:11.929
We have our LLMs that we wrote in previous notebooks

00:04:12.128 --> 00:04:14.528
with our climate_llm and our calculator_llm.

00:04:15.128 --> 00:04:16.928
We have the functions that we've been working with,

00:04:17.129 --> 00:04:19.329
climate functions and calculator functions.

00:04:19.672 --> 00:04:21.771
and we have our workflow.

00:04:21.872 --> 00:04:24.872
But now we have added an evaluation config section.

00:04:25.172 --> 00:04:27.271
Eval is a top-level section,

00:04:27.471 --> 00:04:31.557
and within eval we can add several evaluations sets.

00:04:31.557 --> 00:04:33.658
First, we add a general property here

00:04:33.658 --> 00:04:36.057
that tells us where to store our evaluation data,

00:04:36.458 --> 00:04:38.658
whether to clean up that evaluation data after a run

00:04:38.658 --> 00:04:40.408
and where our datasets come from.

00:04:40.408 --> 00:04:42.158
And now we can list our evaluators.

00:04:42.358 --> 00:04:45.057
There are many different types of evaluations you can run.

00:04:45.358 --> 00:04:46.958
Maybe you have some you've written yourself,

00:04:47.057 --> 00:04:49.358
maybe you're using standard evaluations such as Ragas.

00:04:49.557 --> 00:04:51.207
And that's what we're going to use

00:04:51.207 --> 00:04:52.858
here. So we'll name this answer accuracy.

00:04:53.425 --> 00:04:57.235
and the type is ragas which is built into NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:04:57.435 --> 00:05:01.535
Ragas needs a metric. There are several different metrics such as groundedness.

00:05:01.735 --> 00:05:04.314
In our case, we're going to pick one called AnswerAccuracy.

00:05:04.714 --> 00:05:07.274
And you can find these metrics in our documentation.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.360
How does it evaluate this metric? It needs an LLM

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.360
and so we will go ahead and pass it the pre-configured climate_llm.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:15.959
And that's going to check our accuracy.

00:05:16.459 --> 00:05:18.660
We've seen NAT run and NAT serve,

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.160
and now we're going to see a new command, nat eval.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.360
This takes the config file that we looked at earlier,

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.759
and it runs the evaluations in there against the workflow defined.

00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:30.608
The evaluator is going to write files out

00:05:30.709 --> 00:05:32.908
to the location that we put in the config.

00:05:33.209 --> 00:05:34.959
And so we see here that it's

00:05:34.959 --> 00:05:36.709
written an answer accuracy output JSON file.

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:39.009
Let's see what's inside that file.

00:05:39.218 --> 00:05:41.398
So we can see the evaluation results.

00:05:41.677 --> 00:05:44.017
The average score was zero over one,

00:05:44.317 --> 00:05:46.718
which means that we evaluated very poorly.

00:05:47.398 --> 00:05:50.017
So what was the average temperature in Austria in 1980?

00:05:50.295 --> 00:05:54.095
This was the question. We expected to get 6.8 degrees Celsius,

00:05:54.095 --> 00:05:58.095
but instead we got 9.574 degrees Celsius.

00:05:58.095 --> 00:06:01.095
Therefore, we missed our only evaluation question,

00:06:01.095 --> 00:06:03.394
so we got zero out of one correct.

00:06:03.971 --> 00:06:06.771
Now let's look at the reasoning steps that it took

00:06:06.771 --> 00:06:08.471
in order to get to that wrong answer.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:10.471
So this is a lot of code.

00:06:10.771 --> 00:06:14.071
The evaluator puts out quite large and verbose JSON files

00:06:14.071 --> 00:06:15.821
that will help you track down what

00:06:15.821 --> 00:06:17.571
went right or wrong in the evaluation.

00:06:18.071 --> 00:06:20.170
So this code just parses that JSON file

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.971
so that we can see the results a little bit more cleanly.

00:06:23.494 --> 00:06:26.394
So we can see the reasoning steps here of the evaluation.

00:06:27.293 --> 00:06:29.694
It tried to pull the average temperature in 1980,

00:06:29.694 --> 00:06:33.293
but it didn't even pass the year to our calculate_statistics function.

00:06:33.803 --> 00:06:35.327
And because of that, it got

00:06:35.327 --> 00:06:37.103
an average for the entire climate period

00:06:37.103 --> 00:06:39.103
1950 to 2025.

00:06:39.803 --> 00:06:43.403
However, it gives us a very confident answer back.

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:47.803
And so, if we didn't run evals, we probably would miss this.

00:06:48.004 --> 00:06:51.704
It's not obviously wrong and the agent is confident in its answer.

00:06:52.004 --> 00:06:54.053
But since we have grounded evals that we

00:06:54.053 --> 00:06:56.103
can now run against our agent using NAT,

00:06:56.103 --> 00:06:58.603
we can see that it's incorrect.

00:06:58.954 --> 00:07:01.055
So let's run this with a config

00:07:01.055 --> 00:07:03.055
that more directly tells the agent

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:04.754
to call with the correct year.

00:07:05.954 --> 00:07:08.754
Now that that's run, let's load these evaluation results

00:07:08.754 --> 00:07:10.454
and see that they are correct.

00:07:10.952 --> 00:07:13.153
Now we can see from our evaluation results

00:07:13.153 --> 00:07:15.452
for the one question answer pair we had,

00:07:15.452 --> 00:07:19.252
that we have one correct answer out of one question.

00:07:19.853 --> 00:07:23.452
And we correctly got 6.8 degrees Celsius.

00:07:24.252 --> 00:07:26.088
So in this notebook, we've seen

00:07:26.088 --> 00:07:28.230
how to build an evaluation dataset,

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:30.410
how to run it against our agentic workflow

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:32.029
using NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:34.029
We've looked at a real world use case

00:07:34.029 --> 00:07:36.129
where the agent confidently gave the wrong answer

00:07:36.129 --> 00:07:37.730
and we were able to discover that

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:40.610
through running evals. Then we were able to fix that bug

00:07:40.829 --> 00:07:43.029
and push our agent right back out into production.
